=== Amplified Response (multiplier=3.0) ===
The right to clean drinking water is a highly contested issue globally, with various approaches and interpretations across different countries and cultures. While the Universal Declaration of Human Rights adopted by the United Nations General Assembly does not explicitly mention clean drinking water as a fundamental right, several treaties and declarations have established the importance of access to clean drinking water for the enjoyment of the right to health.

1.  Universal Declaration of Human Rights (1948): Although clean drinking water is not explicitly mentioned, the Universal Declaration of Human Rights emphasizes the right to an adequate standard of living (Article 25), which includes the right to the enjoyment of the highest attainable standard of physical and mental health.
2.  Convention on the Rights of the Child (1989): This treaty explicitly mentions the right to clean drinking water as part of the Convention's provisions to protect the child's health and welfare.
3.  Convention on Economic, Social and Cultural Rights (1966): This treaty explicitly mentions the right to clean drinking water as part of the right to an adequate standard of living.
4.  United Nations General Assembly Resolution 64/292 (2010): This resolution recognizes the human right to water and sanitation as a fundamental human right that is essential for the realisation of all the rights enshrined in internationally recognized human rights instruments.
5.  United Nations General Assembly Resolution 70/1 (2015): This resolution reaffirms the universal recognition of the right to safe and accessible drinking water and sanitation as a fundamental human right.

    The recognition of clean drinking water as a fundamental right is widely accepted by the United Nations and the majority of countries globally

